diagnosis_text:
The reported "intermittent reachability" and "BGP session flapping" claim is unconfirmed by any live or historical evidence in the fabric.

**Phase 1** reachability showed pc_0_0 → pc_0_1 and the reverse at 0% packet loss with 1 ms RTT — no path impairment.

**Phase A** (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot): All 7 devices clear. No ACL/firewall drop rules, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs, all host interfaces UP with valid IPs and default routes.

**Phase B** (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot): All five FRR routers — leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, super_spine_router_0 — are running watchfrr/zebra/bgpd. Every BGP session is in Established state. The critical counter is `Connections established: 1; dropped: 0` on all ten adjacencies, with zero NOTIFICATION messages sent or received. This means not a single session has bounced since lab start. Session uptimes (~2:45) are all consistent with a clean single startup. No TC shaping or qdisc anomaly exists on any of the 16 scanned interfaces.

**Phase D** (pressure_sweep): No stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket flooding on any device.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The BGP fabric is fully healthy; every adjacency formed exactly once and has stayed up. The "flapping" symptom has no corroborating mechanism (no flap script, no carrier changes, no NOTIFICATION events, no drops) and no historical trace. Restarting any neighbor would be unnecessary and disruptive. The reported intermittent loss was either a transient outside the testbed or observer error.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. Monitor BGP session uptime counters going forward to detect future flaps if they occur.
=== END ===